  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nTetracycline Analogues / metformin\n\nChief Complaint:\ndyspnea\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nPrimary Oncologist: ___ \n\nPrimary Care Physician: ___\n \nCC: dyspnea  \n \nHPI:  \n___ COPD, extensive stage SCLC, brain lesion, presents for \nevaluation of worsening dyspnea and now found to have renal \nfailure. \n\nSince finishing chest XRT ___, the patient felt that dyspnea \nhad been improving up until a few days ago. She acutely began to \nhave ___ left flank pain (constant) and increased dyspnea at \nrest and with activity. Her dyspnea is such that she can only \nspeak a few words before catching her breath, and she feels \nconstantly unable to catch her breath even at rest. She denies \ncough, hemoptysis, sputum production, wheeze, chest pain, pain \nwith breathing. She has also noticed that over the past several \ndays, she has felt very dehydrated. She keeps drinking more \nfluids because she has been urinating very infrequently. She has \nalso been sleeping more. She denies any recent fevers, sputum \nproduction. Denies lower extremity swelling or calf pain. \n\nShe was initially seen at ___ and was given \nalbuterol/Atrovent nebulizers, methylprednisolone IV. \nRespiratory status seemed improved after nebulizers, labs show \nworsening renal failure from a baseline creatinine of 0.82 4.0. \nChest x-ray shows a questionable infiltrate, and patient \nreceived levofloxacin 500 mg IV.\n\nIn the ___ ED initial vital signs were: 100.0 110 135/76 22 \n92% 3L. Labs were significant for WBC 5.8 (97% PMN), H/H \n8.5/25.9, plt 129, INR 1.1, Na 137, K 4.7, Cl 103, HCO3 16, BUN \n30, Cr 4.2, glucose 268, lactate 1.9, and venous pH 7.33. Tox \nscreen was negative. UA showed hazy urine with mod leuks, \nnegative nitrites, 36 WBC and few bacteria. CXR demonstrated \nprobable right lower lobe infiltrate. The patient was admitted \nto OMED for pneumonia and ARF.\n\nTransfer vital signs were: 97.7 102 125/70 25 95% Nasal Cannula. \nOn arrival to the floor, patient looked very dyspneic, with \nincreased work of breathing but had no hypoxia. She endorses \nleft flank pain and dyspnea but no other complaints. \n\nShe notes that in her last admission, she did have leg swelling \nand felt better with some diuresis but this admission, denies \nany leg swelling and has lost ___ lbs over several weeks. She \nhas poor appetite. No N/V/changes in bowel habits. No dysuria. \nNo headache. She has not changed her medications except stopping \nLovenox this past month on the advice of her PCP and \ncardiologist.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\nMs. ___ is a ___ year old woman with COPD, extensive stage \nSCLC widely metastatic above and below the diaphragm and to the \nbrain, who presented with worsening dyspnea in the context of \nnew renal failure, found to have 1.4cm kidney stone, improved \nwith percutaneous nephrostomy and IV diruesis. Course \ncomplicated by acute hypoxia during ___ procedure requiring brief \nICU stay and intubation. \n\n# DYSPNEA: Etiology of her dyspnea is most likely secondary to \nanion gap acidosis and volume overload from renal failure. Due \nto new renal failure, she could not undergo CTA Chest. However, \nlower extremity dopplers were negative and she improved with \ndiuresis, making embolism much less likely. She desatted to the \n___ when laid flat for her percutaneous nephrostomy and required \nbrief ICU stay and intuabtion, but was quickly extubated the \nfollowing day and continued to improve on the floor with IV \ndiuresis. \n\n# UTI: Patient was noted to have worsening renal function on \n___ with alterations in mental status. Her UA was grossly \npositive and she was started empirically on Ceftriaxone for \nsuspected urinary source. Urine cultures grew E. coli with \nsensitivities still pending at discharge. She was transitioned \nto PO ciprofloxacin and given a 10 day course to complete at \nhome. \n\n# ACUTE RENAL FAILURE: CT imaging showed that the most likely \ncause of renal failure was uric acid stones (one blocking the \nleft kidney measuring 14mm on CT abdomen), with uric acid \ncrystals seen on microscopy. Course was complicated by UTI as \nabove. She underwent left percutaneous nephrostomy tube \nplacement on ___ with good urine output via her nephrostomy \ntube. Her tube was found to be clogged and was changed out by ___ \non ___. Creatinine trended down from a peak of 5.4 to 1.3 on \ndischarge. Nephrology followed the patient while inpatient and \nrecommended starting allopurinol, with colchicine overlap for 1 \nweek, and sodium citrate to alkalinize the urine. Urology \nfollowed the patient and suggested elective lithotripsy as an \noutpatient. Patient stated that she was not interested in \nlithotripsy as an outpatient and expressed her wish to go home \non hospice care. She was discharged to home to start hospice in \naccordance with her wishes.\n\n# REBOUND TENDERNESS: Improved after resolving her constipation, \nbut still concerning for more sinister process such as ischemia, \ninfection, or partial obstruction. Lactate was normal, no signs \nof infection, and no nausea or vomiting. KUB did not show \nobstruction. At the time of discharge patient did not have any \nabdominal tenderness. \n\n# SMALL CELL LUNG CANCER: management has been per outpatient \noncologist Dr. ___ Dr. ___. This admission patient \ntold us multiple times that she does not want further chemo and \ndoesn't want to \"suffer\". She would still like to be treated for \nreversible problems (diuresis, antibiotics, etc),  but code \nstatus now DNR/DNI, does not want us to \"reverse death\" (see \nfamily meeting note in ___ from ___. Her main priority at \nthis point is to be able to be home with her cats. Outpatient \noncologists were updated throughout the admission and patient \nwas discharged to home with hospice services in accordance with \nher wishes.\n\n# TACHYCARDIA: The patient was noted to have sinus tachycardia \nduring her entire hospital course. At first, this was concerning \nfor pulmonary embolism. However, we noted that she has a history \nof sinus tachycardia as far back as ___. She remained \nhemodynamically stable while inpatient.\n\n# DIABETES II: She was maintained on an insulin sliding scale \nwhile inpatient.\n\n# Adrenal hyperplasia: Increased her home dexamethasone from \n0.5mg daily to 0.5mg BID for stress dose for one week, decreased \nagain when stabilized. \n\nTRANSITIONAL ISSUES:\n-------------------\n# Renal failure: The patient should follow up with Nephrology \nand Urology as an outpatient if she chooses. (appointments are \nscheduled, she may cancel if she wants)\n# CODE: DNR/DNI, but no ICU transfer or painful interventions\n# EMERGENCY CONTACT: ___ (brother) ___\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nPRIMARY DIAGNOSIS:\nAcute renal failure\nLeft hydronephrosis\nUric acid kidney stone\n\nSECONDARY DIAGNOSIS:\nPulmonary edema\nSmall cell lung cancer\nCOPD\nSinus tachycardia\nAdrenal hyperplasia\nDiabetes mellitus type II",
